	{
		version = "0.5.42",
		lines = {
			"/flatui scale 80 now sets the scale it names. At the defaults it did nothing, because \001Scale below 65%\002 (on by default) sets the scale before \001Custom scale\002. From 65 to 115 it now turns \001Pixel-perfect scale\002 and \001Scale below 65%\002 off and uses \001Custom scale\002; from 35 to 64 it uses \001Small scale\002 with Scale below 65% on. Chat then gives the real result in the words of /flatui status, as \"Flat UI: Custom UI scale: 80%. /reload puts the game's scale back.\", and the reload notice shows when Scale below 65% goes off. /flatui scale off answers the same way.",
			"/flatui followed by words that are no command, as /flatui border colour, now opens the options window with those words in \001Search\002: the results show lit, Enter opens the first, and a miss shows the closest pages. Before, it printed the whole command list. /flatui help and /flatui ? still list the commands.",
			"Setup's \001Your screen is set\002 card can now take a \001Finish\002 back until you reload. After a save into the profile in use it shows \001Undo setup\002, which puts that profile back as it was, as \001Bring back\002 does (\"Flat UI: Raid is back as it was before setup.\", with \"Game settings setup changed stay as they are.\" when it wrote some). A Finish that made a new profile now shows \001Back to Default\002, as a ready-made look does. The Finish line in chat ends \"Undo setup on the card, or Profiles > Bring back, puts it back until you reload.\"",
			"Home's \001Needs a reload\002 \001Undo\002 now keeps the change in the record. \001Recent changes\002 shows the Undo as a line of its own, and Ctrl+Z (or that line's \001Undo\002) makes the change again, as the reload notice's Undo does. Before, the change vanished from Recent changes and Ctrl+Z stepped back to an older one. The strip now ends \"Ctrl+Z makes it again.\"",
			"The small reset arrow on a changed row now says what it did in the strip: \"Reset Border colour: back to #000000. Ctrl+Z puts it back.\" (\"back to its default\" where the value has no words of its own). The arrow brightens under the mouse, as the \001Search\002 box's cross does.",
			"The questions and tips of \001Delete profile\002, \001Reset profile\002, \001Copy from\002 and an import with \001Replace\002 no longer say \"for good\". Each now ends \"Bring back puts it back until you reload.\" (or \"them\"), as in 'Delete \"Alt\"? No other character uses it. Bring back puts it back until you reload.' They stay red, and Enter still does not answer them.",
			"Dragging \001Custom scale\002, or changing a row of the \001Fonts\002 page such as \001Text size\002 or \001Interface font\002, in a fight now says so, once a fight, while the options window shows: \"Flat UI: the UI scale changes when the fight ends.\" or \"Flat UI: fonts change when the fight ends.\". The change still lands as the fight ends.",
			"The sidebar now shows where your changes are: a small dot in the accent after the name of each module whose pages (its tabs and \001Per frame\002) hold a setting off its default. The dot goes once the last of them is back at its default. The \001Icon rail\002 layout shows no names and no dots.",
			"The \001Flat UI\002 page now has a preview band: setup's bordered panel with a health and a mana bar, in your border, background and \001Bar texture\002. Its \001Colours\002 tab shows setup's colour bars: health at 100%, 70%, 40% and 15%, the cast, channel, uninterruptible, interrupted and craft colours, reaction and power. Both follow each change at once.",
			"\001Switch automatically\002: \001Switch now\002 in a fight now waits for the fight's end, as a profile picked by hand does. The strip says \"Switches to Raid when the fight ends.\", the title bar shows \"Raid after the fight\", and the profile switches as the fight ends. Before, it only said \"Profiles change after combat.\" and nothing waited.",
			"New \001Fade as one runs out\002 (on), under \001Squares for your heals over time\002. Each square now fades from full to about a third over the last 3 seconds of its heal over time, so a healer sees which Renew or Rejuvenation to refresh next. It goes back to full when the spell is recast. In a fight it fades by the end time read before the pull, and a square that never ends stays full. Setup's Bex shows the last square half faded.",
			"Heal over time squares on party frames draw at the default \001Square size\002 6 again at one pixel a unit. Since 0.5.41 they drew as 5, which left almost half less colour inside the rim. A square now stops one pixel short of the bar's edge, so sizes 7 to 14 picked for raid cells still stay inside the 10-unit party bar. Setup's Bex matches.",
			"New \001Five-second rule\002 (off), on the \001Player frame\002 tab. After each spell that costs mana, a thin bright spark crosses your mana bar from left to right over 5 seconds and starts again at the next cast. When it reaches the end, Spirit is refilling your mana again. It works in a fight, on the game's bar or Flat UI's, and a druid in Bear or Cat Form sees it on the druid mana bar. Test mode loops it, and setup's \001Player and target frames\002 step draws it 40% of the way across the mana bar.",
			"\001Dim members out of range\002 now darkens only the member's own picture, name row and bars (their body in \001One frame\002). Before, it also darkened the top of their debuff icons and pet frame, which left a half-dark strip. A party pet's frame now dims by its own range. With \001Border for your target\002 at Own colour, the outline wraps the same part, just clear of the picture's border, and no longer runs through the pet frame and the debuff row.",
			"With \001Picture\002 None in \001Separate parts\002, as the \001Healer\002 look sets it, the party marks now use the empty picture square. The raid marker sits at its top, the resurrection icon at full size in its middle and AFK at its bottom, clear of the name row and the health text. In \001One frame\002, where None removes the square, they stay on the health bar.",
			"\001AFK on party frames\002 now fades in and out. After the first minute it counts the time away, \"AFK 3m\" and later \"AFK 1h\", from when Flat UI first saw the member go, so a group waiting at the boss knows whether to wait or pull.",
			"New \001AFK on raid frames\002 (on). Raid cells and raid-style party frames show the same soft grey AFK, fading and counting the same way, where the game's status text sits and at its size. It works in a fight too and hides while the member is dead or offline. Setup's \001Boss and raid frames\002 step shows it on Eli.",
			"Setup's \001Party frames\002 step now draws \001AFK on party frames\002. Dru, already drawn out of range, shows a grey AFK at the bottom of his picture, or at the left of his bar with \001Picture\002 None in \001One frame\002, and pointing at the row brackets it. The row moved there from \001Auras, flags and icons\002.",
			"Setup: \001Role icons for\002 moved to the \001Party frames\002 step. Cole, Dru and Fay lose their icons with Tanks and healers, and Mara loses hers with Healers only. The bracket is on Bex's healer icon, which every choice keeps. Before, picking a choice in \001Auras, flags and icons\002 changed nothing in its drawing.",
			"Some changes made in a fight now say once in chat, while the options window is open, that they apply when the fight ends, as the heal over time squares already did. This covers \001Dim members out of range\002 (\"Flat UI: party members dim by range when the fight ends.\"), \001Low health border\002, a \001Health text\002 or \001Power text\002 format, and the party and raid marks: the AFK words, \001Resurrection icon on party frames\002, the raid markers, the leader icons and \001Border for your target\002 (\"Flat UI: party and raid frame marks change when the fight ends.\"). Each change still applies at the end of the fight. A change raised with the window closed, such as zoning or a new member, stays silent.",
			"New \001Flash as you change target\002 (on), under \001Target and focus\002 on the \001Marks and icons\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002: as you change target, the new target's health bar border flashes once in \001Target colour\002. It is a thicker outline that fades over about a third of a second and leaves the normal target border, also in a fight, so with Tab in a pack you see at once which nameplate it took. Setting a focus flashes the focus's nameplate in \001Focus colour\002. Setup's Target and Focus samples flash as the drawing opens and as the row turns on.",
			"Rogues: the Overpower row is now \001Overpower, Mongoose Bite or Riposte on enemy nameplates\002. A rogue who knows \001Riposte\002 sees its icon pop over their target's nameplate as it becomes usable after they parry. It glows for its 5 seconds (a parry of yours renews them), is grey while only energy is short, follows a change of target and goes as you cast it, with the same swipe, pop and fade as Mongoose Bite. Setup's \001Marks and icons\002 drawing shows it for a rogue who knows Riposte.",
			"New \001Nameplates on enemy pets and totems\002 (off), \001Nameplates on minor enemies\002 (off) and \001Friendly players as names only\002 (off), under \001Which show\002 on the \001Size and clicks\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002. These are the game's own settings, so the clutter of totems and hunter pets, or friendly players' health bars, can go without leaving Flat UI. Each shows the game's value and writes nothing until you move it, as the other game rows of that tab do.",
			"Setup's \001Nameplate auras\002 and \001Nameplate text\002 steps, and the preview bands of the \001Auras\002 and \001Text\002 tabs, now draw \001Show auras\002 and \001Show health text\002. At \001Only on the target\002 the auras and the health number stay on a \001Your target\002 sample alone, and the plain enemy beside it goes bare. \001On the target and focus\002 adds a \001Focus\002 sample that keeps them. Pointing at either row brackets the sample that keeps them.",
			"Setup's \001Marks and icons\002 drawing for a rogue or druid has a second combo sample, \001Full points\002: all five squares, in \001Full points colour\002 while \001Full points stand out\002 is on. Dragging the colour repaints it at once, the row pops together as \001Full points stand out\002 turns on, and pointing at either row brackets it.",
			"Setup's \001Nameplate colours and threat\002 step and the \001Threat\002 tab's preview band now draw a fifth sample, \001Held by another tank\002, while you count as a tank with \001Enemies held by another tank\002 on. It is in \001Another tank's colour\002, fades like an enemy held for you, has no ring at \001When the enemy needs you\002 and shows 60%. Pointing at \001Enemies held by another tank\002 or \001Another tank's colour\002 brackets it.",
			"Fix: with \001Your role\002 at \001From your role, stance or form\002, setup's threat samples now work your role out afresh (group role, Main Tank, a tank aura, Defensive Stance, Bear Form, Righteous Fury). They show the colours your nameplates will take after \001Finish\002, not those of the role you had picked before.",
			"Fix: turning \001Restyle friendly nameplates\002 off or on in a fight now says in chat, once a fight while the options window shows, \"Flat UI: nameplates take their new size when the fight ends.\", as the other size rows do. Before, the change seemed to do nothing until the fight ended.",
			"With \001Show auras\002 at \001Only on the target\002 or \001On the target and focus\002, each change of target or focus now eases the old row of auras out and the new one in over 0.15 s, as the threat rings and combo points fade, so Tab no longer makes the debuff rows blink. A nameplate that shows and a change of the options still set them at once.",
			"Tips for the \001Nameplates\002 rows that had none: \001Restyle friendly nameplates\002, \001Own border and background\002, \001Incoming heals\002, \001Absorb shields\002, \001Heal absorbs\002, \001Colour health bars by health left\002, \001Only in dungeons and raids\002, \001Show threat value\002, the three \001Enemy's target\002 rows, \001Level box shape\002, \001Level box gap\002, \001Class icon size\002, \001Raid marker\002, \001Arrow for nameplates behind you\002, \001Shield on uninterruptible casts\002, \001Aura opacity\002, \001Another tank's colour\002, \001Extra clickable width\002 and \001Extra clickable height\002. Each says in one sentence what it changes.",
			"Cast bars: the spell icon left of your cast bar and your pet's, with \001Spell icon on player and pet bars\002, now stays on the bar through its finish fade and its 1.3 s Interrupted hold, with or without \001Flat fill\002, so you still see which spell was kicked. Before, it went the moment the cast ended, and no icon showed during the Interrupted hold. The latency zone still goes as the cast ends.",
			"\001Target of enemy casts\002: the \"> Bex\" past the target or focus cast bar now stays while the bar fades or holds Interrupted, so you can still read who the kicked Polymorph was aimed at. It goes as the bar hides or the next cast starts. Setup's picture and test mode keep the icon, the name and the pushback in the Interrupted state too, as the live bars do.",
			"New \001Seconds until your interrupt is ready\002 (on), on the \001Text and icon\002 tab of \001Cast bars\002: while an enemy you target or focus casts and your Kick, Counterspell or Pummel cools down, a small \"4\" counts down inside the left end of its cast bar in \001Interrupt on cooldown colour\002. You see at a glance whether Kick is back before the 3.5 s Fireball lands, and the number goes as the bar turns \001Interrupt ready colour\002. It works with or without \001Flat fill\002, in a fight too, while \001Interrupt readiness on enemy casts\002 is on, and setup's \001Focus\002 row draws a sample.",
			"New \001Channel tick colour\002 (white at first) under \001Channel ticks on the player bar\002: the tick marks of Mind Flay, Arcane Missiles or Drain Life take it at 60% opacity on the live bar, in setup's picture and in test mode. A priest with \001Class colour on the player bar\002 can now pick a dark colour that shows on the white bar.",
			"Combat text: with \001Spell names\002 on \001After every spell\002, a named number in the Outgoing area now takes a lane as wide as its name, and only the two lanes away from your character. An Arcane Explosion, Multi-Shot, Consecration or Hellfire tick on four enemies now reads as two clean columns instead of names printed over each other, and the rest wait their turn.",
			"New \001Sound when your focus casts\002 (None at first, with the same eight choices), under \001Sound\002 on the \001Cast bars\002 page: it plays as the enemy you focus starts a cast or channel while your interrupt is ready and in range, at most once a second, so you hear \"kick now\" without watching the focus bar. It needs \001Interrupt readiness on enemy casts\002, and picking a sound plays it once.",
			"Combat text: test mode's Outgoing sample and /flatui combat test now write \"824 Fireball\" (the client's own name of the spell) while \001Whose hits\002 is \001Yours\002 and \001Spell names\002 is \001After every spell\002, as setup's picture does. Pointing at \001Spell names\002 in setup now marks the 510 Fireball sample.",
			"Combat alerts: \001Low health\002 and \001Low mana\002 now breathe in step with \001No target\002, one shared cycle, instead of dimming while the other brightens. As the fight ends they fade out over a quarter second with No target instead of vanishing in one frame, so \001-Combat\002 pops in a calm screen.",
			"Test mode: the first sample Interrupted of the player cast bar in a session now plays your \001Sound when you are interrupted\002 once, so you can judge it with the bar in view. None plays nothing.",
			"Tips on \001Flat fill\002, \001Class colour on the player bar\002, \001Own colours on target and focus bars\002, \001Border takes the cast colour\002, \001Target and focus bar icon\002 and the four custom cast bar sizes on the \001Cast bars\002 pages, and on \001Flat fill\002, \001Fill from the right\002 and \001Out of range opacity\002 of \001Swing timers\002. Each now says in one line what it does and what depends on it.",
			"And 41 more changes in the full changelog on CurseForge.",
